 this can be hours of downtime. Strategies:",[1229,1230,1231,1237,1251],"ol",{},[819,1232,1233,1236],{},[173,1234,1235],{},"Add a new column, backfill, swap, drop old"," — minimizes lock time.",[819,1238,1239,1246,1247,1250],{},[173,1240,1241,1242,1245],{},"Use ",[33,1243,1244],{},"USING"," to convert"," — ",[33,1248,1249],{},"USING col::newtype"," lets you control the conversion.",[819,1252,1253,1256],{},[173,1254,1255],{},"Plan ahead"," — pick the right type at creation.",[18,1258,1260],{"id":1259},"truncate","TRUNCATE",[14,1262,1263,1265,1266,1269],{},[33,1264,1260],{}," empties a table instantly — far faster than ",[33,1267,1268],{},"DELETE"," (no per-row work, no trigger firing by default):",[23,1271,1272],{"language":25},[27,1273,1275],{"className":29,"code":1274,"language":25,"meta":31,"style":31},"TRUNCATE orders;                       -- empties the table\nTRUNCATE orders, customers;            -- truncate multiple\nTRUNCATE orders RESTART IDENTITY;      -- also reset SERIAL sequences\nTRUNCATE orders CASCADE;               -- also truncate tables with FKs to this one\n``\n::\n\n`TRUNCATE` is **not transactional in MySQL** (implicit commit), but **is transactional in PostgreSQL** (can be rolled back). `CASCADE` follows foreign keys — be careful, it can empty tables you didn't name.\n\n## CREATE TABLE LIKE — copy structure\n\n::code-wrapper{language=\"sql\"}\n```sql\n-- Copy structure (no data)\nCREATE TABLE orders_archive (LIKE orders INCLUDING ALL);\n\n-- Copy structure with data\nCREATE TABLE orders_2024 AS\n  SELECT * FROM orders WHERE ordered_on >= '2024-01-01';\n``\n",[33,1276,1277,1287,1297,1315,1325,1330,1335,1339,1401,1405,1410,1414,1419,1424,1429,1434,1438,1443,1448,1469],{"__ignoreMap":31},[36,1278,1279,1281,1284],{"class":38,"line":39},[36,1280,1260],{"class":42},[36,1282,1283],{"class":53}," orders;                       ",[36,1285,1286],{"class":237},"-- empties the table\n",[36,1288,1289,1291,1294],{"class":38,"line":57},[36,1290,1260],{"class